1
00:00:00,433 --> 00:00:02,100
A single drop of water.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:05,033
A rope dragging over stone. Then silence.

3
00:00:05,033 --> 00:00:06,833
Somewhere above the Nile,

4
00:00:06,833 --> 00:00:09,567
Egypt’s greatest kings lie together in

5
00:00:09,567 --> 00:00:12,567
a place no one was meant to find. Tonight,

6
00:00:12,567 --> 00:00:15,100
follow the sounds they left behind.

7
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:17,033
Not just a story you watch,

8
00:00:17,033 --> 00:00:18,700
but a journey you hear.

9
00:00:18,700 --> 00:00:21,500
How did pharaohs with magnificent tombs

10
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:24,267
end up hidden in one dark passage?

11
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:27,867
Think of Ramesses the Great. Temples.

12
00:00:27,867 --> 00:00:29,033
Colossal statues.

13
00:00:29,033 --> 00:00:31,100
A name carved into stone as if

14
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:33,100
it could outlast time itself.

15
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:34,867
He had a royal tomb in the

16
00:00:34,867 --> 00:00:36,800
Valley of the Kings. So why,

17
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:39,800
when modern explorers searched for his body,

18
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:41,933
was his resting place no longer

19
00:00:41,933 --> 00:00:44,067
where it should have been?

20
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:47,367
Gold could be stripped from a coffin.

21
00:00:47,367 --> 00:00:49,567
Amulets could be torn from bandages.

22
00:00:49,567 --> 00:00:51,567
A sealed doorway was an obstacle,

23
00:00:51,567 --> 00:00:52,967
not always a deterrent.

24
00:00:52,967 --> 00:00:54,667
When the old kingdom’s power

25
00:00:54,667 --> 00:00:55,667
began to weaken,

26
00:00:55,667 --> 00:00:58,100
even the resting places of famous rulers

27
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:00,367
could no longer be taken for granted.

28
00:01:00,367 --> 00:01:01,933
Someone had to decide what

29
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:03,567
could still be saved.

30
00:01:04,633 --> 00:01:06,500
By the end of the New Kingdom,

31
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:08,567
Egypt was living through political

32
00:01:08,567 --> 00:01:11,233
and economic strain. Authority was divided,

33
00:01:11,233 --> 00:01:12,767
resources were contested,

34
00:01:12,767 --> 00:01:15,133
and the royal cemeteries around Thebes

35
00:01:15,133 --> 00:01:16,900
were increasingly vulnerable.

36
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,300
The kings who had once commanded armies

37
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:21,600
could no longer defend even their own

38
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:24,667
bodies. The task fell to people still alive.

39
00:01:25,700 --> 00:01:26,333
In Thebes,

40
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:29,067
powerful priests of Amun held responsibility

41
00:01:29,067 --> 00:01:31,400
for sacred institutions and the dead.

42
00:01:31,400 --> 00:01:34,033
They did not have the luxury of rebuilding

43
00:01:34,033 --> 00:01:35,900
every plundered tomb. Instead,

44
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:38,267
they began an extraordinary operation:

45
00:01:38,267 --> 00:01:39,833
checking ancient burials,

46
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:41,467
repairing damaged remains,

47
00:01:41,467 --> 00:01:44,033
and moving some of Egypt’s most important

48
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:45,867
rulers away from danger.

49
00:01:46,933 --> 00:01:48,700
Imagine the strange sight.

50
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:51,367
A king who had been buried with enormous

51
00:01:51,367 --> 00:01:54,133
ceremony is brought into the light again.

52
00:01:54,133 --> 00:01:57,100
His linen is examined. His coffin is opened.

53
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:59,133
Broken wrappings are replaced.

54
00:01:59,133 --> 00:02:00,800
This is not a coronation.

55
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:02,167
It is not a victory.

56
00:02:02,167 --> 00:02:04,433
It is something almost unheard of:

57
00:02:04,433 --> 00:02:06,533
a second chance at burial.

58
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:10,233
The priests left something more valuable

59
00:02:10,233 --> 00:02:12,633
than the gold that was disappearing:

60
00:02:12,633 --> 00:02:14,400
information. Short records,

61
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:16,267
written on coffins or linen,

62
00:02:16,267 --> 00:02:18,000
noted names and movements.

63
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:19,900
These little inscriptions are

64
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:21,300
often called dockets.

65
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:23,667
They tell us that some royal mummies

66
00:02:23,667 --> 00:02:25,333
travelled more than once.

67
00:02:25,333 --> 00:02:26,933
And each new destination

68
00:02:26,933 --> 00:02:28,733
had to be kept secret.

69
00:02:29,767 --> 00:02:31,333
Was this simply a rescue?

70
00:02:31,333 --> 00:02:33,700
Much of the evidence points to genuine

71
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:35,400
concern for the royal dead.

72
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:37,833
Yet there is another side to the story.

73
00:02:37,833 --> 00:02:40,300
Some coffins had their gold and precious

74
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:42,067
materials carefully removed.

75
00:02:42,067 --> 00:02:44,567
Researchers still debate how protection,

76
00:02:44,567 --> 00:02:45,500
religious duty,

77
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:47,667
and the need for valuable resources

78
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:49,100
became entangled.

79
00:02:50,133 --> 00:02:52,400
It is tempting to divide the people of

80
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:54,700
ancient Egypt into heroes and thieves.

81
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:57,133
History rarely lets us do that so easily.

82
00:02:57,133 --> 00:02:59,533
The same authorities who preserved royal

83
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:01,767
bodies may also have reclaimed costly

84
00:03:01,767 --> 00:03:03,500
materials from their burials.

85
00:03:03,500 --> 00:03:04,867
Whatever their motives,

86
00:03:04,867 --> 00:03:06,133
the mummies survived.

87
00:03:06,133 --> 00:03:08,400
The next problem was finding somewhere

88
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:09,833
thieves would not.

89
00:03:10,900 --> 00:03:11,967
West of the Nile,

90
00:03:11,967 --> 00:03:14,067
the cliffs of Deir el-Bahari rise

91
00:03:14,067 --> 00:03:15,567
steeply from the desert.

92
00:03:15,567 --> 00:03:17,833
Below them stands the great mortuary

93
00:03:17,833 --> 00:03:19,100
landscape of Thebes,

94
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:21,300
including the temple of Hatshepsut.

95
00:03:21,300 --> 00:03:23,567
The rock looks bare from a distance.

96
00:03:23,567 --> 00:03:25,000
That was its advantage.

97
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:27,167
A royal resting place could vanish

98
00:03:27,167 --> 00:03:29,433
into the mountain without a monument

99
00:03:29,433 --> 00:03:30,833
pointing the way.

100
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:33,900
The place we now call TT320 was

101
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:36,000
reached through a deep shaft and

102
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:37,767
passages cut into the rock.

103
00:03:37,767 --> 00:03:40,233
Getting a coffin inside was difficult.

104
00:03:40,233 --> 00:03:42,033
Getting many inside demanded

105
00:03:42,033 --> 00:03:43,333
planning and labour.

106
00:03:43,333 --> 00:03:45,500
But once the entrance disappeared

107
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:47,067
beneath debris and sand,

108
00:03:47,067 --> 00:03:48,667
this inconvenience became

109
00:03:48,667 --> 00:03:50,400
a form of protection.

110
00:03:51,433 --> 00:03:53,767
The hiding place was not an empty vault

111
00:03:53,767 --> 00:03:55,467
waiting for forgotten kings.

112
00:03:55,467 --> 00:03:57,433
It was associated with the burial

113
00:03:57,433 --> 00:03:58,833
of Pinedjem the Second,

114
00:03:58,833 --> 00:04:00,133
a high priest of Amun,

115
00:04:00,133 --> 00:04:01,700
and members of his family,

116
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:02,833
including Neskhons.

117
00:04:02,833 --> 00:04:05,300
Their own funerary world became connected

118
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:07,933
with rulers who had lived centuries earlier.

119
00:04:07,933 --> 00:04:09,400
What began as one burial

120
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:11,000
story grew into many.

121
00:04:12,033 --> 00:04:14,633
Among the dead were kings whose names

122
00:04:14,633 --> 00:04:17,033
still echo across history: Ahmose,

123
00:04:17,033 --> 00:04:18,800
Thutmose the Third, Seti,

124
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:21,900
Ramesses the Second, and Ramesses the Third.

125
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:23,033
Queens, priests,

126
00:04:23,033 --> 00:04:25,500
and other members of elite families

127
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:27,300
shared the crowded refuge.

128
00:04:27,300 --> 00:04:29,567
More than fifty individuals were

129
00:04:29,567 --> 00:04:31,400
associated with the cache.

130
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:33,733
No royal architect had originally

131
00:04:33,733 --> 00:04:36,267
imagined a gathering like this.

132
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:38,767
Empires rose and fell.

133
00:04:38,767 --> 00:04:40,800
Travellers admired the temples.

134
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:43,000
Scholars copied inscriptions from

135
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:44,633
monuments they could see.

136
00:04:44,633 --> 00:04:47,367
Yet a few steps away, beneath the cliffs,

137
00:04:47,367 --> 00:04:49,400
an almost impossible collection

138
00:04:49,400 --> 00:04:50,467
remained hidden.

139
00:04:50,467 --> 00:04:53,000
Egypt’s most famous rulers had become,

140
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,233
in a strange way, missing persons.

141
00:04:55,233 --> 00:04:57,633
The next clue would come from people

142
00:04:57,633 --> 00:04:59,767
who knew the mountain well.

143
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:02,600
During the nineteenth century,

144
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:04,767
members of the Abd el-Rassul family,

145
00:05:04,767 --> 00:05:07,000
who lived near the Theban necropolis,

146
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:08,800
came to know the burial place.

147
00:05:08,800 --> 00:05:11,200
Exactly how the first discovery happened

148
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:12,900
is not securely established.

149
00:05:12,900 --> 00:05:15,033
Yet one story became so famous that,

150
00:05:15,033 --> 00:05:16,000
for many people,

151
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,933
it is now inseparable from the Royal Cache.

152
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:20,867
In that story,

153
00:05:20,867 --> 00:05:22,700
a goat wanders away and falls

154
00:05:22,700 --> 00:05:23,967
into a dark opening.

155
00:05:23,967 --> 00:05:25,633
Its owner hears the animal,

156
00:05:25,633 --> 00:05:27,200
climbs down to rescue it,

157
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:29,167
and finds something far greater

158
00:05:29,167 --> 00:05:30,233
than a lost goat.

159
00:05:30,233 --> 00:05:31,967
It is an unforgettable tale.

160
00:05:31,967 --> 00:05:33,933
We should treat it as a legend,

161
00:05:33,933 --> 00:05:36,100
not as a proven eyewitness account.

162
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:37,733
What happened afterward is

163
00:05:37,733 --> 00:05:39,400
much easier to trace.

164
00:05:40,467 --> 00:05:43,467
Funerary objects carrying royal names began

165
00:05:43,467 --> 00:05:46,367
surfacing in the Luxor antiquities market.

166
00:05:46,367 --> 00:05:48,733
Papyrus scrolls, burial equipment,

167
00:05:48,733 --> 00:05:51,367
and inscriptions hinted at a discovery

168
00:05:51,367 --> 00:05:53,867
no official excavation had reported.

169
00:05:53,867 --> 00:05:55,967
To an ordinary buyer they were

170
00:05:55,967 --> 00:05:58,800
remarkable curiosities. To investigators,

171
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:00,900
they suggested a question with

172
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:02,567
unsettling consequences:

173
00:06:02,567 --> 00:06:05,267
where was all of this coming from?

174
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:08,400
By the late 1870s and early 1880s,

175
00:06:08,400 --> 00:06:10,067
the trail drew attention to

176
00:06:10,067 --> 00:06:11,633
the Abd el-Rassul family.

177
00:06:11,633 --> 00:06:13,233
The authorities wanted the

178
00:06:13,233 --> 00:06:14,600
source of the objects.

179
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:17,067
The family had reasons to remain silent.

180
00:06:17,067 --> 00:06:19,300
Their knowledge could be profitable,

181
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:21,467
but it could also bring punishment.

182
00:06:21,467 --> 00:06:22,100
For years,

183
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:24,200
the mountain kept its secret while

184
00:06:24,200 --> 00:06:26,233
suspicion grew in the city.

185
00:06:27,267 --> 00:06:28,767
The investigation was not

186
00:06:28,767 --> 00:06:30,167
a neat detective story.

187
00:06:30,167 --> 00:06:32,300
Accounts of the case describe harsh

188
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:34,000
interrogations and coercion.

189
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:36,300
The details differ between retellings,

190
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:38,100
and there is no reason to turn

191
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:39,633
suffering into spectacle.

192
00:06:39,633 --> 00:06:41,567
What matters is that the secrecy

193
00:06:41,567 --> 00:06:42,600
eventually broke.

194
00:06:42,600 --> 00:06:44,900
The authorities were finally shown the

195
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:48,233
entrance to a tomb beyond anything they expected.

196
00:06:49,267 --> 00:06:51,833
On the sixth of July, eighteen eighty-one,

197
00:06:51,833 --> 00:06:54,467
the official party reached the Royal Cache.

198
00:06:54,467 --> 00:06:55,367
Gaston Maspero,

199
00:06:55,367 --> 00:06:57,933
head of the antiquities service, was away.

200
00:06:57,933 --> 00:07:00,633
Émile Brugsch and the Egyptian scholar Ahmed

201
00:07:00,633 --> 00:07:02,900
Kamal took part in the investigation.

202
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:04,300
The entrance was small.

203
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:05,900
The descent was dangerous.

204
00:07:05,900 --> 00:07:07,667
Then the darkness opened into

205
00:07:07,667 --> 00:07:09,600
a passage full of coffins.

206
00:07:10,667 --> 00:07:12,867
Try to stand there for a moment.

207
00:07:12,867 --> 00:07:15,467
You are underground, the air is close,

208
00:07:15,467 --> 00:07:17,333
and your light moves across

209
00:07:17,333 --> 00:07:18,700
coffin after coffin.

210
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:21,167
The decorations look ancient even by

211
00:07:21,167 --> 00:07:23,433
the standards of an ancient land.

212
00:07:23,433 --> 00:07:26,000
Then one royal name comes into focus.

213
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:27,100
Another follows.

214
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:29,900
The discovery is no longer merely a tomb.

215
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:33,200
It is an entire chapter of Egypt’s history.

216
00:07:34,267 --> 00:07:36,700
Ramesses the Second. Seti the First.

217
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:38,000
Thutmose the Third.

218
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,267
Rulers separated by centuries now

219
00:07:40,267 --> 00:07:42,300
lay within one hidden complex.

220
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:45,300
The find offered an answer to the mystery of

221
00:07:45,300 --> 00:07:48,067
bodies missing from their original tombs.

222
00:07:48,067 --> 00:07:51,000
The priests’ extraordinary plan had worked.

223
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:52,567
And almost immediately,

224
00:07:52,567 --> 00:07:54,067
a new threat appeared:

225
00:07:54,067 --> 00:07:57,067
what should be done with the discovery?

226
00:07:58,133 --> 00:07:59,433
The site was vulnerable,

227
00:07:59,433 --> 00:08:01,633
and officials feared further disturbance.

228
00:08:01,633 --> 00:08:04,000
The response was swift: remove the contents.

229
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:05,933
Coffins and objects were brought out

230
00:08:05,933 --> 00:08:07,500
in roughly forty-eight hours.

231
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:09,500
It was an astonishing feat of labour,

232
00:08:09,500 --> 00:08:11,100
carried out with the tools and

233
00:08:11,100 --> 00:08:12,367
conditions of the time.

234
00:08:12,367 --> 00:08:14,500
It also meant that the tomb could not be

235
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:16,700
documented as it should have been.

236
00:08:17,733 --> 00:08:19,833
Imagine trying to record the precise

237
00:08:19,833 --> 00:08:22,100
position of every coffin while a narrow

238
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:23,933
shaft is being cleared in haste.

239
00:08:23,933 --> 00:08:25,100
Details were missed.

240
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:26,600
Some objects were damaged.

241
00:08:26,600 --> 00:08:28,467
The original arrangement of much

242
00:08:28,467 --> 00:08:30,967
of the cache was lost before archaeologists

243
00:08:30,967 --> 00:08:31,833
could study it.

244
00:08:31,833 --> 00:08:34,167
The royal bodies had been rescued again,

245
00:08:34,167 --> 00:08:36,000
but part of their story vanished

246
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:37,400
during the rescue.

247
00:08:38,467 --> 00:08:41,000
The remarkable cargo was transported toward

248
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,333
Cairo and the museum at Bulaq. The Nile,

249
00:08:43,333 --> 00:08:45,167
which had carried stone, grain,

250
00:08:45,167 --> 00:08:46,767
and the wealth of pharaohs,

251
00:08:46,767 --> 00:08:48,867
now carried the pharaohs themselves.

252
00:08:48,867 --> 00:08:50,567
The bodies were out of danger

253
00:08:50,567 --> 00:08:51,500
in the mountain.

254
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:52,867
Yet removing them posed

255
00:08:52,867 --> 00:08:54,167
a different challenge:

256
00:08:54,167 --> 00:08:56,167
understanding who each person was,

257
00:08:56,167 --> 00:08:58,167
and how they had come there.

258
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:01,000
Not every mummy lay in a coffin

259
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:02,433
made for that individual.

260
00:09:02,433 --> 00:09:04,433
Some had been moved several times,

261
00:09:04,433 --> 00:09:06,333
with wrappings replaced and names

262
00:09:06,333 --> 00:09:08,000
recorded over older surfaces.

263
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:10,467
A coffin could preserve one identity while

264
00:09:10,467 --> 00:09:12,300
its occupant told another story.

265
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:14,700
Scholars had to read the clues carefully.

266
00:09:14,700 --> 00:09:16,700
One remarkable cache was becoming a

267
00:09:16,700 --> 00:09:18,500
lifetime of separate mysteries.

268
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:19,833
And the royal roll call

269
00:09:19,833 --> 00:09:21,400
was still incomplete.

270
00:09:22,467 --> 00:09:24,033
In eighteen ninety-eight,

271
00:09:24,033 --> 00:09:26,367
a second great royal mummy cache came

272
00:09:26,367 --> 00:09:28,633
to light in the Valley of the Kings,

273
00:09:28,633 --> 00:09:30,867
in the tomb of Amenhotep the Second,

274
00:09:30,867 --> 00:09:31,767
known as KV35.

275
00:09:31,767 --> 00:09:34,133
Its discovery added another collection

276
00:09:34,133 --> 00:09:36,033
of rulers and royal relatives.

277
00:09:36,033 --> 00:09:38,600
The Royal Cache at Deir el-Bahari had not

278
00:09:38,600 --> 00:09:41,333
been the only secret refuge after all.

279
00:09:42,400 --> 00:09:42,967
Together,

280
00:09:42,967 --> 00:09:45,400
the caches changed how the modern world

281
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:47,533
could study the Egyptian monarchy.

282
00:09:47,533 --> 00:09:50,200
Inscriptions could be compared with bodies.

283
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:52,600
Coffins could be examined. Eventually,

284
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:55,167
new scientific methods would look beneath

285
00:09:55,167 --> 00:09:57,767
ancient wrappings without destroying them.

286
00:09:57,767 --> 00:10:00,467
Yet every answer still depended on evidence

287
00:10:00,467 --> 00:10:02,767
that had survived thousands of years,

288
00:10:02,767 --> 00:10:05,367
and on how carefully it was handled.

289
00:10:06,433 --> 00:10:07,967
For some of these rulers,

290
00:10:07,967 --> 00:10:10,433
their remains became subjects of medical

291
00:10:10,433 --> 00:10:11,967
imaging and conservation.

292
00:10:11,967 --> 00:10:14,500
Researchers could ask about age, illness,

293
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:16,900
injuries, and mummification techniques.

294
00:10:16,900 --> 00:10:19,433
But even modern technology cannot recover

295
00:10:19,433 --> 00:10:21,700
an object’s exact position after that

296
00:10:21,700 --> 00:10:23,433
position was never recorded.

297
00:10:23,433 --> 00:10:25,633
The Royal Cache is therefore both an

298
00:10:25,633 --> 00:10:28,267
archaeological triumph and a warning.

299
00:10:29,333 --> 00:10:31,767
On the third of April, twenty twenty-one,

300
00:10:31,767 --> 00:10:33,667
the story entered a new chapter.

301
00:10:33,667 --> 00:10:36,267
Cairo watched a carefully planned procession

302
00:10:36,267 --> 00:10:38,567
carry twenty-two royal mummies from the

303
00:10:38,567 --> 00:10:40,867
Egyptian Museum in Tahrir Square to the

304
00:10:40,867 --> 00:10:43,300
National Museum of Egyptian Civilization.

305
00:10:43,300 --> 00:10:45,100
Eighteen kings and four queens

306
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:46,700
travelled through the city,

307
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:49,233
centuries after their first funerals.

308
00:10:50,267 --> 00:10:52,133
These twenty-two mummies had

309
00:10:52,133 --> 00:10:53,733
not all come from TT320.

310
00:10:53,733 --> 00:10:55,533
Some were discovered there;

311
00:10:55,533 --> 00:10:57,333
others came from the second

312
00:10:57,333 --> 00:10:58,667
royal cache at KV35.

313
00:10:58,667 --> 00:11:01,367
Among the celebrated names were Ramesses

314
00:11:01,367 --> 00:11:03,367
the Second and Seti the First.

315
00:11:03,367 --> 00:11:05,233
The journey was not a return

316
00:11:05,233 --> 00:11:06,833
to their original tombs.

317
00:11:06,833 --> 00:11:08,833
It was a new way of protecting

318
00:11:08,833 --> 00:11:11,133
and presenting their history.

319
00:11:12,167 --> 00:11:13,667
At the National Museum of

320
00:11:13,667 --> 00:11:15,000
Egyptian Civilization,

321
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,300
visitors could meet these rulers under

322
00:11:17,300 --> 00:11:19,267
carefully controlled conditions.

323
00:11:19,267 --> 00:11:21,567
The faces once hidden behind stone and

324
00:11:21,567 --> 00:11:23,567
linen had become part of a public

325
00:11:23,567 --> 00:11:25,500
conversation about Egypt’s past.

326
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:27,067
The crowds were different,

327
00:11:27,067 --> 00:11:28,533
the light was different,

328
00:11:28,533 --> 00:11:31,067
and the guardians now worked with science.

329
00:11:31,067 --> 00:11:32,567
One part of the old story

330
00:11:32,567 --> 00:11:34,200
had come full circle.

331
00:11:35,233 --> 00:11:37,333
Think back to the priests writing

332
00:11:37,333 --> 00:11:40,033
small notes on old coffins. A name. A date.

333
00:11:40,033 --> 00:11:41,233
A record of a move.

334
00:11:41,233 --> 00:11:43,133
They could not have known that

335
00:11:43,133 --> 00:11:45,300
strangers thousands of years later

336
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:47,633
would read those marks. Without them,

337
00:11:47,633 --> 00:11:49,967
some of the people inside the coffins

338
00:11:49,967 --> 00:11:51,767
might have remained unknown.

339
00:11:51,767 --> 00:11:53,833
The smallest sounds of their work

340
00:11:53,833 --> 00:11:55,967
have travelled the farthest.

341
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:58,667
There are still questions the

342
00:11:58,667 --> 00:12:00,200
mountain refuses to settle.

343
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:01,700
Exactly when was each body

344
00:12:01,700 --> 00:12:02,867
placed in the cache?

345
00:12:02,867 --> 00:12:04,967
What did the priests intend when they

346
00:12:04,967 --> 00:12:07,333
stripped precious materials from coffins?

347
00:12:07,333 --> 00:12:09,867
How much of the original arrangement did the

348
00:12:09,867 --> 00:12:12,000
nineteenth-century clearance destroy?

349
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,233
The story survives, but not every page.

350
00:12:14,233 --> 00:12:16,767
That uncertainty is part of its power.

351
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:19,667
We began with missing kings.

352
00:12:19,667 --> 00:12:22,067
Now we know where many of them went,

353
00:12:22,067 --> 00:12:24,300
why their burials were disturbed,

354
00:12:24,300 --> 00:12:27,233
and how a hidden refuge helped them survive.

355
00:12:27,233 --> 00:12:28,767
The Royal Cache was not

356
00:12:28,767 --> 00:12:30,233
a palace for the dead.

357
00:12:30,233 --> 00:12:31,900
It was a desperate answer

358
00:12:31,900 --> 00:12:33,300
to a dangerous world.

359
00:12:33,300 --> 00:12:35,333
Its great treasure was not the

360
00:12:35,333 --> 00:12:36,800
gold that disappeared.

361
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:39,200
It was the people who remained.

362
00:12:40,267 --> 00:12:42,833
Listen once more: linen against linen,

363
00:12:42,833 --> 00:12:44,200
a rope across stone,

364
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:45,867
a quiet mark on a coffin.

365
00:12:45,867 --> 00:12:47,767
No single person planned the

366
00:12:47,767 --> 00:12:50,333
three-thousand-year journey from royal

367
00:12:50,333 --> 00:12:53,033
tomb to mountain cache to modern museum.

368
00:12:53,033 --> 00:12:54,600
Yet the traces connect.

369
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:56,800
The pharaohs did not escape time.

370
00:12:56,800 --> 00:12:58,633
Someone kept carrying their

371
00:12:58,633 --> 00:12:59,800
names through it.

372
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:01,867
And perhaps that is the closest

373
00:13:01,867 --> 00:13:03,433
thing to eternity.

